2026-08-25T00:00:00Z (RFD66606-2059) 'Bad news reports lead with the bottom line
 # uuid: 9fcd2f74-64b1-55e2-b756-aaec5533d40d
 # tags: rfd:title
 RFD:Prediscussion  1 RFD
 Serials:VSekaiFabric

2026-08-25T00:00:01Z (RFD66606-2059) 'A status report opens on its outcome, then gives the why and the next step.
 # tags: rfd:hook
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:02Z (RFD66606-2059) 'A status report carries an outcome the reader acts on: a slipped date, a broken build, a failed smoke. A report that opens with progress and buffers the outcome to the end makes the reader scan for the catch before the report says anything they can use.
 # tags: rfd:intrigue
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:03Z (RFD66606-2059) 'Check that a status report opens on its outcome, with the explanation and the next step after it.
 # tags: rfd:exit
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:04Z (RFD66606-2059) 'Bad news reports lead with the bottom line
 # tags: rfd:details-title, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:05Z (RFD66606-2059) 'See `DETAILS.md` for the full argument.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:06Z (RFD66606-2059) 'See `DETAILS.md` for the full argument.
 # tags: rfd:related
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:07Z (RFD66606-2059) 'The problem
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:08Z (RFD66606-2059) 'How does a report in this project phrase an outcome the reader needs first?
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:09Z (RFD66606-2059) 'Context and problem statement
 # tags: rfd:madr-heading
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:10Z (RFD66606-2059) 'A status report carries an outcome the reader acts on: a slipped date, a broken build, a failed smoke. A report that opens with progress and buffers the outcome to the end makes the reader scan for the catch before the report says anything they can use. How does a report in this project phrase an outcome the reader needs first?
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:11Z (RFD66606-2059) 'Decision drivers
 # tags: rfd:madr-heading
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:12Z (RFD66606-2059) '- A reader who learns the outcome in the first sentence starts deciding on it immediately, rather than after a paragraph of buildup.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:13Z (RFD66606-2059) '- An executive or on-call reader scans the opening line and stops; the rest is there for whoever needs the why.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:14Z (RFD66606-2059) '- The rule stays mechanical enough to apply in review without debate.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:15Z (RFD66606-2059) '- Urgency, a down service, a failed gate, leaves no room for a warm-up.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:16Z (RFD66606-2059) 'Considered options
 # tags: rfd:madr-heading
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:17Z (RFD66606-2059) '- Indirect approach: open with a progress buffer, then state the outcome.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:18Z (RFD66606-2059) '- Direct approach (bottom line up front): state the outcome first, then the explanation, then the next step.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:19Z (RFD66606-2059) 'Decision outcome
 # tags: rfd:madr-heading
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:20Z (RFD66606-2059) 'Chosen option: "the direct approach", because the reader holds the outcome before the explanation, so the explanation reads as context rather than as suspense, and a reader who needs only the outcome stops after one line.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:21Z (RFD66606-2059) 'A status report carries three parts in order:
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:22Z (RFD66606-2059) '1. The bottom line comes first. The opening sentence states the outcome plainly, the slip, the failure, the result, with no buffer, no apology, and no warm-up. A reader who reads only this line knows where the work stands.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:23Z (RFD66606-2059) '2. The explanation follows. It gives the factual reason for the outcome; the reader already holds the result, so the explanation carries the why without holding it back.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:24Z (RFD66606-2059) '3. The next step closes the report: the fix underway, the alternative open to the reader, or the time of the next update.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:25Z (RFD66606-2059) 'The voice rules out two habits:
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:26Z (RFD66606-2059) '- A progress buffer that opens on what went well and defers the outcome to the end.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:27Z (RFD66606-2059) '- An apology or hedge in place of the outcome in the first sentence.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:28Z (RFD66606-2059) 'A good outcome takes the same shape: the first sentence states the result, and the rest carries the why and the next step.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:29Z (RFD66606-2059) 'Consequences
 # tags: rfd:madr-heading
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:30Z (RFD66606-2059) '- Good, because the reader decides on the outcome from the first line instead of scanning for it.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:31Z (RFD66606-2059) '- Good, because one shape covers a slip, a failed gate, and a shipped result alike.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:32Z (RFD66606-2059) '- Good, because an urgent report, a down service, a recall, leads with the fact that drives the response.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:33Z (RFD66606-2059) '- Bad, because the opening reads blunt, and an author used to a buffer needs practice to drop it.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:34Z (RFD66606-2059) 'Confirmation
 # tags: rfd:madr-heading
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:35Z (RFD66606-2059) 'Review checks that a status report opens on its outcome, with the explanation and the next step after it, and no progress buffer or apology ahead of the bottom line.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:36Z (RFD66606-2059) 'More information
 # tags: rfd:madr-heading
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:37Z (RFD66606-2059) 'This applies the BLUF (bottom line up front) convention to status reports, and sits alongside the tenseless continuous-present voice (`rfd/2025-tenseless-continuous-present-voice`) for prose across the repo.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2059  1 LINE
 Text:Written
